{"type": "item.completed", "item": {"id": "item_11", "type": "command_execution", "command": "/bin/sh -lc 'cat /tmp/research_history/episode_11_continuing_notes.md /tmp/research_history/episode_10_continuing_notes.md /tmp/research_history/episode_9_continuing_notes.md'", "aggregated_output": "# Continuing research notes: episode 11 complete\n\nThe selected source is **671_episode11_point_gemm_columns_point_recurrence_residual_increments**, restored byte-for-byte to `/workspace/solution/method.py`. No evaluation is pending.\n\n- Best complete official raw: **0.036028623731787474 seconds**; score: **0.93091276060350758**.\n- Exact evaluator source hash: **c84af4a358d47b35c429d0e0a5a14f2f3efc0ea14a2ae1c341aea6f4fa25e404**.\n- All 30 cases and 270 quality records pass. Worst normalized RMSE 0.0070255387888521932; minimum Spearman 0.99995660399517805; minimum top-quarter overlap 0.99199999999999999.\n- Frozen launcher SHA256: **bca6ef61c3a1bc0ded2aa6b2da4734b62072846def679d12a2d7ae669fc88bd1**. Invoke `sh ./solve.sh`; launcher mode is frozen.\n- Ordinary submission files are exactly method.py and solve.sh; method size 12421 bytes.\n\nRetention uses the fastest complete official quality-valid aggregate, including the historical best. Local timings are diagnostic only. The starting source644 is identical to the prior episode winner; a faster repeat of those bytes is timing variation, not an algorithmic gain.\n\n## Complete official measurements\n\n| Source | Raw seconds | Score | Exact evaluator source hash |\n|---|---:|---:|---|\n| 644_episode11_start554_repeat | 0.03667739993397845 | 0.92966868985772533 | a73d8c270d13c6defb541b70facabbcf7f909dd3911b6a774e200d531691823d |\n| 651_episode11_class_persistent_fold_final_delta | 0.059034108334647804 | 0.8867982411585742 | ce0bae80f347f153ec795682e50c3580e959af7cfd70cdea5be4d43fe279ffb0 |\n| 656_episode11_point_recurrence_residual_increments | 0.036817420866282188 | 0.92940019057386136 | 42450446e384142251d975fffa7fec856cdde17780fd71b1e6d309ef3e30dd3d |\n| 671_episode11_point_gemm_columns_point_recurrence_residual_increments | 0.036028623731787474 | 0.93091276060350758 | c84af4a358d47b35c429d0e0a5a14f2f3efc0ea14a2ae1c341aea6f4fa25e404 |\n| 686_episode11_class_unique_centered_point_increments_gemm | 0.044770907000929581 | 0.91414886138872065 | ea02e4397ee9d379a0011887f5117ee8ae57606d9b61fa30d067c94569c50313 |\n\nAll original evaluator stdout and reports are archived unchanged in each source directory. No own numerical workloads ran concurrently with official evaluations. The first persistent-residual source651 had temporary slow case measurements concentrated in seed303, but it loses under the required aggregate and was reverted.\n\n## Retained recipe\n\nThe selected source uses persistent hard class residuals, direct point log-marginal increments and point column GEMM reductions. It retains the class vectorized schedule, the final reference-only class extrapolation0.25, the point schedule[3:-3], analytic batch initialization and exact row-balanced coupling expectation.\n\n## New mechanisms and decisions\n\n- **645–651: rank-two residuals and persistent hard class geometry.** Rank-two addmm/baddbmm forms C-f-g in one native call; aliasing and separate-output controls all pass but are slower. Keeping R=C-f-g in-place over the hard suffix gives df=rowmin(R)-eps*lb, dg=colmin(R)-eps*la, then R-=.5*df+.5*dg. Final g extrapolation folds into a .75 reference shift. All30 pass. Fresh diagnostics favored651, but its full official result lost. This is a new dataflow mechanism, distinct from merely reusing an allocated direct-transform buffer.\n- **652–653: one-sided class padding.** Pad candidate supports only; sort actual reference supports and use segmented row reductions with native column reductions. Both physical layouts pass all30 but are slow (~.092–.100 local means), rejecting the hoped-for benefit from reduced padding.\n- **654–658: mean-dual state and direct increments.** After hard residual activation, scalar mean-dual sums suffice for the class objective; scalar controls pass but reductions add overhead. Direct class and point log-marginal increments avoid readding then cancelling current potentials; all pass.656 full official loses narrowly.\n- **659–664: native power/reduction fusion.** The last point stage can use sum(K^4)=norm4(K)^4=norm2(K^2)^2, cancelling roots directly in logs if desired. All pass, but fresh timings reject the native norm paths. Square-root kernel storage with norm2 marginals also passes and is slower (~.0725).\n- **665–668: reuse first point kernel for coupling.** Exact temperature conversion and unary/shift cancellation pass but are slower (~.0516). Same-temperature, dyadic and quarter-step temperature approximations fail all30, worst nRMSE .2275/.2591/.1503. Correct coupling regularization is necessary. Initial unscreened builder omitted the scalar shift factor; original bytes are saved as method_initial.py and the corrected builder is explicit.\n- **669–674: point dispatch combinations and isolated controls.** Column GEMM reductions combine with persistent class residuals and direct increments; all pass.671/672 were close to baseline in fresh diagnostics;671 received the complete official evaluation. Pure norm/increment point controls with original class geometry also pass.\n- **675–677: certified cached minima on resident residuals.** Track winner indices and outside-neighbor lower bounds through additive shifts; refresh only uncertified rows/columns with full top-two minima. All pass with baseline accuracy but are slower (.0558–.0699). This confirms that certificate/gather overhead remains expensive even without repeated residual construction.\n- **678–680: NumPy only for the hard class suffix.** Torch handles coarse soft transforms; NumPy views handle in-place hard residuals, hard reductions and optionally final transforms. All pass. Fresh timings provide no speed win. Keeping just NumPy minima is cheaper than the full NumPy suffix.\n- **681–688: centered initial class geometry.** Exact gauge control forms original C-f0-g0, then direct centered products replace that construction. R0=-(a-Ea)·(b-Eb)+Ea·Eb-E||a||²/2-E||b||²/2. Solve from zero duals and restore2mean(C) to the objective.684 obtains this scalar from the offset and guards the actual exponent range;685 centers twenty unique supports before packing and shares unique means/norms.686 combines this with point increments and column GEMM.687 vectorizes the twenty unique statistics in two padded slices and688 combines that with retained point dispatch. All pass. See complete local screens and official reports for final decisions.\n\nFull equations and tradeoffs are in `episode11_derivations.md`. These mechanisms retain all empirical supports, original contiguous1024 point batches and the selected finite annealing recipe. There are no learned parameters, output tables, input caches, solver I/O or private material.\n\n## Evidence and continuation\n\n- 45 numbered source snapshots; 44 complete all30 local screens; 41 locally valid sources; 3 numerical failures; 5 complete official evaluations.\n- Builders round1–11, manifests, per-case quality screens, five fresh-process timing rounds (180 independent calls), failures, actual reports and working notes are in `/tmp/research_history`.\n- `episode_11_index.json` records source hashes and evidence. `episode11_official_summary.json`, `episode_11_final_selection.json` and `episode11_final_audit.json` preserve selection and exact bytes. `python /tmp/research_history/episode11_index.py --restore` reproduces restoration.\n- New synthetic stress uses seeds202/505, uniform labels/Gaussian016/mixed, three independent public-array permutations each. Canonical000 freshly recomputes targets from those permitted arrays. Original records and reproducible RNG seeds are in episode11_shuffle_stress.json/.log; these are diagnostics, not protected targets.\n\nStress summaries: [{\"source\": \"644_episode11_start554_repeat\", \"cases\": 18, \"valid\": true, \"worst_nrmse\": 0.0075730066088854076, \"min_rho\": 0.999971762663529, \"min_top\": 0.9946666666666667}, {\"source\": \"671_episode11_point_gemm_columns_point_recurrence_residual_increments\", \"cases\": 18, \"valid\": true, \"worst_nrmse\": 0.007572877982644854, \"min_rho\": 0.9999717635524179, \"min_top\": 0.9946666666666667}, {\"source\": \"686_episode11_class_unique_centered_point_increments_gemm\", \"cases\": 18, \"valid\": true, \"worst_nrmse\": 0.00757263611914155, \"min_rho\": 0.999971762663529, \"min_top\": 0.9946666666666667}, {\"source\": \"688_episode11_class_vector_centered_point_increments_gemm\", \"cases\": 18, \"valid\": true, \"worst_nrmse\": 0.007572653232128127, \"min_rho\": 0.999971762663529, \"min_top\": 0.9946666666666667}]\n\nNext episode should start from the selected exact bytes. Persistent class residuals and centered initial geometry are useful new building blocks; confirm gains in full official measurements before retaining combinations. Native vector norms, certified minima and one-sided segmentation were slower. Read prior episode summaries before revisiting sparse graphs, coresets, lower precision, polynomial coupling, generic grouping or aggressive schedule truncation: these families have extensive failure/slow controls already.\n\nNo protected assets, evaluator code/targets, private inputs, credentials, environment variables, hidden tests, other trajectories, network, external installs or subagents were accessed. Only method.py changed as a submission asset; solve.sh remains frozen. Research artifacts remain outside the submission directory.\n\nFinal decision: centered candidate686 passed all30/all270 but measured0.04477090700092958 seconds, score0.9141488613887206. It loses to671;671 is restored byte-for-byte. Final diagnostic profile ran after all evaluations with arguments/imports loaded before timing:0.06785737500467803 seconds, class0.026, point0.020, coupling0.008. Profile timing includes instrumentation and is not an evaluator score. Dense class geometry and point kernels remain the main costs. Source and launcher hashes were verified again after profiling. No evaluation is pending.\n# Continuing research notes: episode 10 complete\n\nCurrent method is the unchanged episode 9 winner, source554, repeated as565 this episode. No new candidate beat its complete official measurement. Current `/workspace/solution/method.py` matches the preserved source and evaluator hash byte-for-byte. No evaluation is pending.\n\n- Best official raw: **0.03685865550020632 seconds**; score: **0.9293211204698644**.\n- Exact evaluator source hash: **a73d8c270d13c6defb541b70facabbcf7f909dd3911b6a774e200d531691823d**.\n- All30cases and all270qualityrecords pass. Worst nRMSE0.007025775756825209, minimum Spearman0.9999565986618442, minimum top-quarter overlap0.992.\n- Frozen solve.sh SHA256: **bca6ef61c3a1bc0ded2aa6b2da4734b62072846def679d12a2d7ae669fc88bd1**. Invoke `sh ./solve.sh`; mode0444 is frozen.\n- Submission ordinary files are exactly method.py and solve.sh; method size11541bytes. Generated workspace method cache removed after final profiling.\n\nThe same source's previous historical report554 measured0.037601041732220133 and score0.92789754637728683. Its faster repeat565 is timing variation, not a new algorithmic improvement. All selections use complete actual official quality-valid aggregates, including the historical best; local timing is never treated as a score.\n\n## Complete official episode10 results\n\n| Source | Raw seconds | Score | Exact evaluator source hash |\n|---|---:|---:|---|\n| 565_episode10_start554_repeat | 0.036858655500206318 | 0.92932112046986437 | a73d8c270d13c6defb541b70facabbcf7f909dd3911b6a774e200d531691823d |\n| 571_episode10_point_gemm_columns | 0.037166569266507091 | 0.92873067571004619 | 11ab757b0756b88114ae8a0842f61511bb2edea6af6a9970fcf3cb01e904deff |\n| 591_episode10_point_gemm_columns_class_tile20 | 0.038183655467582865 | 0.92678034648338203 | a81f881c4909fe7a4c7746449c8aa8f2b08ef266c8a5a5cf63c4259bd964d654 |\n| 620_episode10_class_and_point_augmented_geometry | 0.048834316631352216 | 0.90635700800034547 | 36f6be7911e35c677ae394915c4ccfdc84eafbf796dba9fde9cfc194641d605b |\n| 627_episode10_augmented_full_cost_contiguous_pairs | 0.037873630533188894 | 0.92737483954592048 | e82bb0d4d623f828bebcd1673a651e4acb54a0edf07f99dcfc761a1d0b911b00 |\n| 642_episode10_full_augmented_class_factor_initialization | 0.03851975833482963 | 0.92613584728118692 | a40a6a5de217330ae595311ed7e92968bcf86f213346651d8c314223a84d0193 |\n\nAll six reports pass all30cases/all270records. Original stdout and JSON reports are archived unchanged inside the source directories. Exact reported source hashes match archived bytes. No own solver numerical work ran concurrently with official evaluations.\n\n## Retained recipe\n\nThe selected source preserves all empirical class supports and contiguous1024point batches. It uses class column-major geometry, vectorized float32 annealing and guarded initial unshifted exponentials. Class loop ends three stages early with reference-only residual extrapolation0.25. Point schedule remains[3:-3], with a reference-only final retained update and complete final candidate c-transform. Shared batch statistics initialize all point potentials analytically. Coupling cancels the exact first row factor and computes mean(rowenergy/rowmass) after the linearized second column factor; its right operands are2D columns to dispatch GEMM. Final point column factors cancel into vector logarithms. No learned parameters, output tables, input caches, solver I/O or private material.\n\n## New hypotheses and outcomes\n\n- **566–584, cache blocks and marginal dispatch:** point recurrence tiles64/128/256/512rows, tiled initial residual exponentials, class tiles10/20/50pairs, and point/class sums replaced by GEMM/BMM against ones. All19sources pass all30. Small tiles and class BMM are slow. Point-column GEMM571 and its class20pair combination591 looked faster in fresh diagnostics, but official0.03716657/0.03818366 lost to565.\n- **585–590, cost allocation and coupling controls:** label matrix as addmm bias/output passes but is slower in fresh processes. Explicit temporary lifetime control also passes. Half/full quadratic column reciprocal correction and exact second reciprocal pass but worsen worst nRMSE to0.0071915/0.0074416/0.0073406. They establish no additional accuracy budget.\n- **591–613, layout, exponential basis, fusion and buffers:** exp/exp2, actual column-major point kernels, Torch einsum/outer scaling, final-point tiles and reusable direct residual buffers. Five generated sources595–597/604/606 have fallback indentation errors; preserved unchanged with all30import-failure records. Corrected609–613 compile and pass. Column-major point kernels are slower, means0.046–0.052. Both buffers613 are sometimes faster locally but have no retained full official report.\n- **614–621, exact cost factors:** packed22dimensional point factors create complete feature/norm/label costs in one GEMM. Controls change accumulation order, retain only12dimensional feature factors, pad to32, or combine point GEMM columns. Class homogeneous12dimensional BMM and combinations also pass. Combination620 was consistent in fresh benchmarks but official0.04883432 lost.\n- **622–624, exact nearest-neighbor trees:** augment reference features with sqrt(2*(max_g-g)), query candidates with zero last coordinate separately per reference label, then add empirical label offsets. This computes the full final c-transform with zero query approximation and no support deletion. All30pass, but means0.0559–0.0586 are slower. Reject for speed.\n- **625–639, shared cost construction and combinations:** one augmented GEMM per reference batch, one full cost GEMM with contiguous original-pair copies, strided views,2048row construction blocks, and combinations with class geometry/tiles or residual buffers. All15pass. Full-cost627 had fresh median0.0341846 but official0.03787363 lost. Gains do not reliably add.\n- **640–643, factor-derived initialization:** batch means of22dimensional point factors compute exact initial potentials; masked12dimensional class means/BMM compute class initial potentials. All30pass with only float32 reduction-order changes.642 was strongest in final fresh median0.0355864, but official0.03851976 lost.\n\nExact factorization: A=[-X,||X||²/2,1,L[y,:]], B=[R,1,||R||²/2,onehot(ry)], so C=A @ B.T. L is computed from current empirical class transport. This changes cost construction, not the exponential kernel or transport supports. Full derivations, including blocking and trees, are in `episode10_derivations.md`.\n\n## Validation and artifacts\n\nFour isolated fresh-process benchmark rounds contain192calls: six alternating rounds over three public cases, eight sources per round. All timings and output std are archived in episode10_freshround1–4.json/.log. Timing noise is substantial. These comparisons selected candidates for full evaluation and are not formal scores.\n\nSources565/613/620/642 pass18independently permuted public-array diagnostics each. Saved canonical000 recomputes every synthetic target from the permuted permitted arrays. Worst nRMSE about0.0085131, minimum Spearman0.99995404 and top overlap0.994667. No tuning against these permutations. Script, seed formula,72records and summaries are in episode10_stress.py and episode10_shuffle_stress.json/.log; these are own public-input calculations, not protected targets.\n\n-79source snapshots;78complete all30local screens;73valid local sources and five preserved import failures; six complete official evaluations.\n- Builders1–13, manifests, screens, benchmarks, failed attempts, reports, derivations and working notes are in `/tmp/research_history`.\n- `episode_10_index.json` records every source/hash and evidence. `episode10_official_summary.json` and `episode_10_final_selection.json` preserve selection. `episode10_final_audit.json` confirms exact source bytes, frozen launcher and all270valid records.\n- Run `python /tmp/research_history/episode10_index.py --restore` to reproduce best restoration. The detailed notes are in `episode_10_detailed_notes.md`; the prior episode notes are preserved as `episode_9_continuing_notes.md`.\n\nFinal selected-source profile loaded arguments before timing and ran after all evaluations:0.04342821seconds total, class0.016, point0.013, coupling0.006. Original profile is in episode10_final_profile.log. This is diagnostic only. Class geometry and dense point kernels remain the main costs.\n\nNext episode should start from exact selected bytes. New augmented cost/dataflow variants have plausible local gains but no established official improvement. Revisit only with a concrete new mechanism or justified paired control. Exact trees are slower. Earlier episodes extensively cover failed or slower sparse graphs, coresets, reduced precision, cumulants, polynomial coupling and aggressive schedule truncation; read prior summaries before repeating them.\n\nNo protected assets, evaluator code/targets, private cases, credentials, environment variables, hidden tests, other trajectories, network, external installs or subagents were accessed. Only method.py changed during submission experiments; frozen launcher and pre-existing protected directories remain untouched.\n# Continuing research notes: episode 9 complete\n\n## Best fully evaluated state\n\n- Best: **554_episode9_shared_coupling_cancel_gemm_columns**, raw **0.037601041732220133 seconds**, score **0.92789754637728683**.\n- Exact evaluator source hash: **a73d8c270d13c6defb541b70facabbcf7f909dd3911b6a774e200d531691823d**.\n- All 30 cases and all 270 quality records pass. Worst normalized RMSE **0.007025775756825209**, minimum Spearman **0.99995659866184416**, minimum top-quarter overlap **0.99199999999999999**.\n- Current `/workspace/solution/method.py` is restored byte-for-byte to this measured source. No evaluation is pending.\n- Frozen solve.sh SHA256: **bca6ef61c3a1bc0ded2aa6b2da4734b62072846def679d12a2d7ae669fc88bd1**. Invoke `sh ./solve.sh`; the frozen launcher has mode0444.\n\nPrevious historical best432 was raw0.03942903473265081, score0.924392250394403, hash7d3f1dd18cf04a39aba10066b90def10384a1f4fd0f9b5562d00d89e1639a354. The new selected method reduces that measured aggregate by 4.6362%. All selection decisions use complete official valid aggregates, including historical best; diagnostic timing is never treated as a score. Timing noise remains substantial.\n\n## Complete official episode9 measurements\n\n| Source | Raw seconds | Score | Exact evaluator source hash |\n|---|---:|---:|---|\n| 497_episode9_start432_repeat | 0.049396719331950105 | 0.90527856408578766 | 7d3f1dd18cf04a39aba10066b90def10384a1f4fd0f9b5562d00d89e1639a354 |\n| 515_episode9_class_column_major | 0.040048826466954776 | 0.92320375925906029 | d55f04d527fb23000cd61107bdbd3facd172e52bb43a5969e195a87f3ae463d3 |\n| 547_episode9_class_column_shared_vector_zero | 0.038594802833298067 | 0.92599194455346467 | 592df6dad60e09f62656181a75ed1687b9e58c4dfa0e8ad799e2a3f4217a33b4 |\n| 554_episode9_shared_coupling_cancel_gemm_columns | 0.037601041732220133 | 0.92789754637728683 | a73d8c270d13c6defb541b70facabbcf7f909dd3911b6a774e200d531691823d |\n| 564_episode9_coupling_lowrank_cost_energy_label_fields | 0.061441357034103326 | 0.88218218453581054 | cec185be3b526a4ac4beb2d90522f8f89d08d13022ae21f1ccdac22f06d9ee73 |\n\nAll reports passed all 30 cases. Original evaluator stdout and reports are archived unchanged inside each source directory. No own numerical work ran concurrently with official evaluations. The baseline repeat497 used exactly the saved432 source.\n\n## Retained mechanisms and numerical recipe\n\nAll empirical supports, contiguous1024 point batches, class/point finite annealing, late class extrapolation, and the linearized second coupling column refinement remain derived from supplied arrays. No learned parameters, output tables or input caches are used.\n\n547 combines column-major class geometry (batched matrix product with reversed physical axes), in-place distance assembly, vectorized annealing schedules/active flags, guarded initial soft-kernel shift removal, shared analytic point initialization, and exact final point column-factor cancellation in vector logarithms. Schedule construction uses the effective np.arange step `(start+delta)-start` and float32 temperatures. The initial unshifted exponential is allowed only when `(max(f)+max(g))/epsilon<60`; positive-support checks retain stable dense fallbacks.\n\n554 adds exact first coupling row-scale cancellation. Let K=exp(-10*C/max(C)), v=1/(m*column_sum(K)), u=1/(n*(K @ v)), and w=v*clamp(2-m*v*(K.T @ u),.01). The exact current expectation equals mean(((K*C) @ w)/(K @ w)), because the first row factor cancels in final row normalization. Two-dimensional right operands invoke faster GEMM kernels; bare GEMV and single-batch BMM were much slower. Full derivation is in episode9_coupling_cancellation_derivation.md.\n\n564 computes the same numerator using the exact low-rank cost factors. One 22-column response holds weighted mass, reference squared norm, ten feature coordinates and ten label-cost fields. Energy is candidate_norm*mass + reference_norm_response - candidate dot feature_response + observed_label_response. The kernel and supports stay exact; only float32 reduction order changes. Its final complete official result was raw 0.061441357034103326, score 0.8821821845358105. It passes quality but is slower, so it is rejected; source 554 remains selected.\n\n## Hypotheses and controlled ablations\n\n- **498/499, unpadded Torch segmented/scatter class reductions:** all 30 pass with base accuracy. Means0.0608/0.0719 seconds are slower; reject for speed. Prior segmented NumPy failures were already covered in episodes4/6.\n- **500–502, final retained class stage:** g-only fails